<%# design.md section-title scale: text-base font-semibold. Was text-2xl, the same
    size as the page h1.

    Optional, because the archived disclosure's <summary> is already the heading for the table
    inside it - a second one under it would announce the same section twice. %>
<%# One root element, deliberately. This partial used to emit the heading, the helper line and the
    table as three siblings, and it is rendered into a `space-y-6` container - whose
    `> :not([hidden]) ~ :not([hidden])` rule outspecifies a plain mt-1 or mt-3. So the heading, the
    line under it and the table each sat 24px apart while the markup said 4px and 12px. A partial
    rendered inside a space-y-* container has to have a single root or the container spaces its
    insides. %>
<section>
  <% if local_assigns[:title].present? %>
    <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900"><%= title %></h2>
  <% end %>

  <%# One line saying what this table is for. Neither table said anything: a reader had "Active
    stocks" and "Archived stocks" and no way to know that one is buyable and the other is not, or
    why a company they remember has moved. design.md's empty states already work this way - say what
    appears here and what to do about it - and a populated table deserves the same courtesy. %>
  <% if local_assigns[:description].present? %>
  <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600"><%= description %></p>
  <% end %>

  <div class="mt-3">
  <%# table-fixed, with a width on every column but the first.
    Active stocks and Archived stocks are two separate <table> elements rendered from this one
    partial, and with the default auto layout each sized its columns from its own content: the
    Buy/Sell pair widened the actions column in the active table and pulled every other column
    left, so the two tables' columns landed in different places (measured at 1366px: column two
    started at 567px in one and 699px in the other). Reading down the page, everything shifted
    sideways at the boundary. table-fixed makes the widths here authoritative, so both instances
    are identical whatever they contain - including an archived table whose rows have one button
    instead of two. %>
  <%= render "shared/table_container", table_class: "table-fixed" do %>
  <thead>
    <tr class="table-header-row">
      <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left">Company (exchange)</th>
      <%# w-40, not w-32: the cell carries a second line under the figure when the price is not the freshest
          the page has - "as of 4 Aug", or "Not priced yet" where a fetch has never succeeded.

          A **Change** column lived here for three commits and is gone. The figure was honest after the job
          stopped fabricating zeros, but it was worth little: the job's cron covers Tuesday to Saturday, so
          "since yesterday" is wrong on a Monday; `fetch_quote` returns nil with no API key, so it is an em
          dash by default; the free tier's rate limit makes a partial update the normal case; and it was
          `hidden lg:table-cell`, so it was absent on the phones these students use. The change that matters
          to a student - what their holding has gained or lost since they bought it - is on the portfolio and
          needs no API for its baseline. See design.md. %>
      <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right w-40">Last price</th>
      <%# policy(Stock).show_holdings?, not current_user.student?, so this agrees with the body
          row in _index_row. show_holdings? also requires a persisted portfolio. Both columns are
          hidden below lg, where their contents move into the primary cell. %>
      <% if policy(Stock).show_holdings? %>
        <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-left hidden w-32 lg:table-cell">Your holdings</th>
        <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right hidden w-52 lg:table-cell">
          <span class="sr-only">Actions</span>
        </th>
      <% end %>

      <%# The staff half of that column. `@class_students&.positive?` as well as the policy check,
          because a column that can only ever say "None" is not a column - the same rule that removed
          the teacher's trailing column of dashes from classrooms#index. A teacher with no classrooms,
          or classrooms where nobody has a portfolio yet, sees the two-column table and no explanation
          of a figure that is not there.

          It is on **every** stocks table, not only the active one, which is what keeps the two stacked
          tables to one column geometry: separate <table> elements size their columns independently, so
          a column present in one and absent in the other moves every other column and the page steps
          sideways at the boundary. An archived stock a student still holds is also exactly the row a
          teacher most wants this figure on. %>
      <% if policy(Stock).show_class_holdings? && @class_students&.positive? %>
        <th scope="col" class="table-header-cell table-header-cell-right hidden w-44 lg:table-cell">Held by</th>
      <% end %>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <%# The freshest price date on this table, which is what a row measures itself against. `.compact.max`
        rather than a date calculation: the newest price we actually hold is the only defensible definition
        of "up to date" when the job's schedule skips two days a week and can fail for any subset of rows. %>
    <% freshest_price_on = stocks.filter_map(&:last_trading_day).max %>

    <% if stocks.any? %>
      <% stocks.each do |stock| %>
        <%= render "stocks/index_row", stock: stock, freshest_price_on: freshest_price_on %>
      <% end %>
    <% else %>
      <%# Five columns with holdings and actions, three without - the Change column counts. An empty row
          that spans the wrong number leaves a gap at the end of the table.

          Above the call, not inside it: a comment tag cannot sit within another tag, because the outer one
          ends at the first closing delimiter it meets. I did that twice in one sitting - once leaking a
          sentence onto the home page and once breaking this partial - which is why
          `no_leaked_template_syntax_test` exists and why it caught the second one. %>
      <%# 4 for a student (holdings and actions), 3 for staff with a Held by column, 2 for anyone else.
          An empty row that spans the wrong number leaves a gap at the end of the table. %>
      <% columns = 2
         columns += 2 if policy(Stock).show_holdings?
         columns += 1 if policy(Stock).show_class_holdings? && @class_students&.positive? %>
      <%= render "admin/shared/empty_row",
                 colspan: columns,
                 icon: "chart-line",
                 title: local_assigns[:title].present? ? "No #{title.downcase} to show" : "Nothing to show",
                 body: "Stocks appear here once your teacher adds them." %>
    <% end %>
  </tbody>
  <% end %>
  </div>
</section>
